4.1 Basics
Wireshark captures packets and shows them in three panes: packet list, packet details (layers), packet bytes.
Capture: pick the interface (eth0 on Kali, or the host-only adapter), start capture, generate traffic, stop, save as .pcapng.
Capture filter (applied before capturing, BPF syntax): host 192.168.56.101, port 21.
Display filter (applied after capture): the ones below.
Useful views: Statistics → Conversations / Protocol Hierarchy / I/O Graph, and Follow → TCP Stream.

4.2 Capturing HTTP, FTP, and DNS
Protocol	Display filter	Notes
HTTP	http	Requests, responses, headers, cleartext forms
FTP	ftp	Commands USER, PASS, RETR, STOR (port 21)
DNS	dns	Queries and responses (UDP 53)

Generate traffic: browse to the Metasploitable2 web server, log in to its FTP (ftp 192.168.56.101), and run nslookup or dig.

Other handy filters: ip.addr == 192.168.56.101, tcp.port == 80, http.request.method == "POST", dns.qry.name contains "example", tcp.flags.syn == 1.

4.3 Finding Credentials in Unencrypted FTP
FTP sends the username and password in plain text.
Filter: ftp.request.command == "USER" || ftp.request.command == "PASS"
Or right-click a packet and choose Follow → TCP Stream to read the whole session.
Lesson: anyone on the network path can read these. Use SFTP or FTPS, HTTPS instead of HTTP, and encrypted protocols in general.
Same applies to Telnet, HTTP basic auth, and POP3 or IMAP without TLS.

4.4 SYN Flood Attack Analysis

What it is: a denial-of-service attack. The attacker sends many SYN packets (often with spoofed source IPs) and never completes the handshake. The server keeps half-open connections in its backlog until the table is full, so legitimate users are refused.

Simulating in the lab (own VMs only):

sudo hping3 -S -p 80 --flood 192.168.56.101           # SYN flood
sudo hping3 -S -p 80 -i u1000 -c 2000 192.168.56.101  # controlled, limited version

(-S = SYN flag, --flood = as fast as possible, -i u1000 = interval in microseconds, -c = packet count.) Using the controlled version is safer for a demo.

Spotting it in Wireshark:

Filter: tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
Signs: a huge number of SYNs in a short time, many from one source (or random sources if spoofed), few or no completed handshakes, repeated SYN/ACKs or RSTs from the server.
Statistics → Conversations shows packet counts per IP. I/O Graph shows the sudden spike.

Defenses: SYN cookies (net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies=1), rate limiting with iptables, reducing SYN timeout, firewalls and IPS, upstream DDoS protection.